592186529777 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529777 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52486476-6056-55af-9404-0c1d6b9322c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530207 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530207 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530207 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd34fb19-51be-53a6-9439-14ce74d4d526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530255 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530255 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530255 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530255 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530255 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "316c9be3-2f1d-573d-ad2e-1f7c5d034777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530385 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530385 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530385 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530385 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e607ce28-3784-5cd7-a847-51fef16dd3b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/72/72fdce3ebf5cbdb9e0b06e88e9fd5a519afdd79faedefd0ec24d003a8bc6e557.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99019613-302f-550e-842d-827f9008365f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530495 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,3)$ and passes through the point $(-3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-3,2),$ we substitute $x=-3$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-3)^{2} & =4p(-3+4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(1) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+3 \\\\ y & =1,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $5$ and  $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7447a9f9-5dea-5ae2-a9c8-6ff307945fcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bade0e6b-46e5-5ab3-8969-32133842f549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186403703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530566 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd16cec3-8893-56c4-81c8-5d657349f57f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5cebdcf-3800-5d31-894a-16ebc8531079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530571 #edb/r